1. Scope
   These terms cover CueLab's source code, program, interface design, cue
   point rules, analysis methods, icons and documentation (the "Software").
   Third-party components included with the Software are not restricted by
   these terms and remain under their own licenses (see section 6).

2. Permitted use
   You may download the official CueLab release from the author free of
   charge, install it on your own computer and use it for your own DJ work
   (including paid performances).

3. Restrictions
   Without the author's prior written permission you may not:
   (a) copy or reproduce all or any part of the Software (source code,
       interface, rules, icons);
   (b) modify the Software or create derivative works based on it;
   (c) distribute, sell, rent, lend or sublicense the Software, or offer it
       for download on any other website or platform;
   (d) reverse engineer, decompile, disassemble or otherwise attempt to
       obtain the Software's source code, algorithms, cue point rules or
       analysis methods;
   (e) use the Software's code, rules or design to develop a product with
       the same or similar functions;
   (f) use the Software's code or output to train artificial intelligence or
       machine learning models;
   (g) remove or change any copyright, license or author notices.
   Source code hosted on GitHub is there for the author's own version
   control, or for reading with the author's explicit permission; this does
   not grant anyone a license to use, copy or modify it.

4. Termination
   If you break these terms, your right to use the Software ends at once and
   you must delete all copies. The author reserves all legal remedies.

5. No warranty
   The Software is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. It backs
   up cues before writing to Serato, but the author is not liable for any
   data loss, wrong cue points, performance problems or other damage. Keep
   your own backups of your music and Serato library.

6. Third-party components
   The Software uses the following open-source components, which are
   provided under their own licenses; these terms do not limit any rights
   you have under those licenses:
   - Beat This! (CPJKU, MIT): beat tracking
   - Demucs (MIT): vocal / drum / bass separation
   - librosa (ISC), NumPy, SciPy (BSD-style)
   - PyTorch / torchaudio (BSD-style)
   - soundfile (BSD-3-Clause) with libsndfile and mpg123 (LGPL-2.1,
     dynamically linked)
   - python-soxr with libsoxr (LGPL-2.1, dynamically linked)
   - pywebview (BSD-3-Clause), PyObjC (MIT)
   The LGPL components ship as separate dynamic libraries; you may replace
   them with compatible versions.
   Where these terms conflict with a third-party license, the third-party
   license governs that component.

7. Contact
   For permission to copy, partnerships, commercial licensing or any other
   use, contact the author through GitHub.
